/* Stagify.ai — the hero CTA's "moving day" scene: the artwork.
 *
 * Every plate scripts/hero-van.js builds is assembled from the strings in this file: a
 * night sky, a far background that differs per furniture style, a second house in the
 * mid band, the "home" — the house on the right, drawn AROUND its own entrance — the road,
 * a streetlamp, the removal van that drives in on hover, and the entrance itself (the door
 * that opens once the van has parked). All inline SVG; no images, no requests.
 *
 * THE STOREY GRID. Every house is drawn on the same 22-unit storey: ground floor y 44–66,
 * upper floor y 22–44, roof above. The entrance art is authored 46 × 44 and rendered at
 * 38 × 36.35 (a uniform 38/46), which puts a door leaf at ≈ 19 units — one storey — so the
 * door never reads as two floors tall. Brick and clapboard courses in the house are
 * generated by COURSES() from the SAME local grid the door art used, so the two can never
 * be out of register (the door art paints no wall of its own — the house shows through).
 *
 * THE ENTRANCE LEAF IS CLIPPED to its resting outline (a <clipPath> on a wrapper outside
 * the transformed group), so when it swings the part that is "inside the house" is cut off
 * at the jamb instead of painting over the frame. `__CID__` in the door strings is replaced
 * per instance by ENT() so ids stay unique when more than one plate is in the document.
 *
 * Colours are literal here rather than CSS tokens on purpose: this is SVG markup built in
 * JS, and the palette is the Enterprise plate's night palette (gold windows #ffd48a) so the
 * three plan CTAs share one light.
 *
 * Authored as prototypes/hero-upload-cta-09 (rounds 4–9 of the exploration); this file is
 * that prototype's art with the page chrome removed.
 */

